WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.334
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.334 --> 00:00:12.060
In Europe in the 16th century,
most people drank beer instead of water.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.140
The brewing process killed all of those
nasty microbes that were found in your

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.000
average 16th century cup of water.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.100
So beer was actually the cheapest
safe beverage available.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.880
When coffee and tea became widely and
cheaply available in Europe,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.740
thanks to the opening of trade routes with
Latin America and east Asia in the 17th

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:38.000
century, they offered a new,
safe alternative to water.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.758
And the subsequent transition from
drinking mostly alcohol to drinking

00:00:42.758 --> 00:00:47.361
mostly caffeine coincided with
the most technologically productive

00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:50.719
period in the continent's history-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:52.060
&gt;&gt; The Industrial Revolution.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.440
I love this correlation
because it illustrates

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.390
everything that I love about history.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.132
History pushes us to be curious
about cause and effect.

00:01:04.132 --> 00:01:07.599
As a kid, I assumed that these
brilliant scientists and

00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:12.469
inventors that I had learned about who
invented the steam engine, who learned

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:16.970
how to harness coal and produce
electricity were solely responsible for

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.430
our being ushered into the modern era.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.870
But studying history, real history, messy
history shows a very different story.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.814
Where the Industrial Revolution was
dependent not only on these brilliant

00:01:31.814 --> 00:01:35.739
inventors and scientists but
also on Europe's transition

00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:39.757
from being permanently drunk
to permanently caffeinated.

00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:43.242
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; History shows us how small changes in

00:01:43.242 --> 00:01:49.270
the way that we live our lives can
trigger massive societal transformation.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.550
Historians study the past to uncover the
unintended consequences of our actions.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.220
We're not always encouraged to think
like historians here at the GSB.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.990
In fact, I think most of us would probably
consider ourselves students of the future.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.940
After all, this place is dedicated to
the things that haven't happened yet and

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.540
the people who are about to dream them up.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.610
But I think that we all
should think like historians.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:21.915
See, I think that Silicon Valley needs
to learn how to think like historians.

00:02:21.915 --> 00:02:26.480
Here, we only consider the past for long
enough to figure out how to disrupt it.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.650
We're not often preoccupied
with unintended consequences.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.630
And so many of the innovations that we've
built are actually causing bigger problems

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.179
than the ones that they set out
to solve in the first place.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.380
To better understand
the impact of our innovation,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.430
we need a better framework for
mapping out cause and effect.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.720
We need to understand
the impacts of our actions.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.378
For example, the National Safety Council
realized that car accidents increased

00:02:57.378 --> 00:03:03.390
by 12.3% over a five-year period due
to an increase in distracted driving.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:08.310
Which is really just a euphemism for not
being able to put your smartphone down.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.310
Or traffic congestion in San Francisco,
it's gotten worse, right,

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.750
it's increased by 60%
over the last ten yers.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.630
And more than half of that is
attributed to Uber and Lyft.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.010
And Airbnb is accused of
decreasing long-term housing stock

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.750
in cities that are already facing housing
shortages and affordability issues.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.510
So how can we learn to
think like historians

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:38.480
here in Silicon Valley to minimize risk
and to mitigate some of this damage?

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:43.624
Well, thinking like a historian means
looking closely at what we're leaving

00:03:43.624 --> 00:03:44.811
behind, first of all.

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:49.370
Historians spend a lot of time digging
through primary source records and

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:54.224
artifacts, which can be broadly
classified into two different categories,

00:03:54.224 --> 00:03:55.490
trash and triumph.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.670
Trash is everything that the people
in the past meant to throw away.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:05.695
Triumph is everything that they
intentionally left behind for us to find.

00:04:05.695 --> 00:04:10.485
And both are equally important
in understanding what life was

00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:13.035
actually like in the past.

00:04:13.035 --> 00:04:18.170
And historians have learned just as much
from ossified food remains as they have

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.550
from the hieroglyphs that are carved in
the walls of the ancient pyramids when it

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.610
comes to understanding what it was
like to live in ancient Egypt.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.700
Now, I would argue that we spend too
much time focused on the triumph and

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:35.110
not enough time looking at the trash when
it comes to understanding our impact here.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.070
Now, sure, the triumph has an interesting
story to tell about our priorities

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.330
as a civilization and a society,

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:48.960
just as the ancient Egyptians built
pyramids to house their dead leader gods.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.689
Jeff Bezos is building a $42 million clock
in the desert as a testament to technology

00:04:54.689 --> 00:04:59.804
that will continue to function for
a millennia, sure, interesting story.

00:04:59.804 --> 00:05:01.965
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:05:01.965 --> 00:05:05.850
the trash has a really
important story to tell, too.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:11.880
The trash tells a story about how our
lives are changing in the most mundane but

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.710
often most fundamental ways.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:19.650
Historians have learned so much about
who was trading with whom by studying

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.390
discarded pottery shards and
tracking them through ancient trash dumps.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.170
And we too can learn
about the behaviors and

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.650
patterns that our
innovations are encouraging

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.060
by studying the things that people
did not intend to leave behind.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.730
What does the refuse in
a ride-share driver's

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:43.060
trunk say about the amount of time
that he has to spend in his car?

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:47.990
What do the deleted direct messages
in a teenage girl's Instagram account

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.550
say about the experience of
being a young woman online?

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.730
In addition to looking at
what we're leaving behind,

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.860
we also have to understand
whom we're leaving out.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:05.022
We need to ask questions about whose
voices are missing from the stories that

00:06:05.022 --> 00:06:06.496
we hear of our impact.

00:06:06.496 --> 00:06:11.790
Historians know all too well that there
is no such thing as a reliable narrator.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:16.610
Everybody's explanation of the fact is
tinged by their own unique perspective.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.306
A horrifying bloodbath and

00:06:18.306 --> 00:06:21.993
a justified seizure of power
are both valid descriptions of

00:06:21.993 --> 00:06:26.578
the French Revolution depending on
where you were standing in 1789.

00:06:26.578 --> 00:06:31.518
The historian's job is to piece
together these multiple perspectives

00:06:31.518 --> 00:06:35.230
to get a comprehensive view
of what happened and why.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:41.400
When it comes to building technology and
developing new ideas, it's easy to lose

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.270
sight of that big-picture view and
get trapped in our own perspectives.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:49.070
Just look at the myriad
tools that we've built

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:53.750
to solve problems that are only
relevant to the urban tech elite.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:58.240
Think an Uber for dog walking app that's
currently valued at $300 million,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.079
seems a little unnecessary.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.110
We need to ask questions
about whose voices

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.380
are missing from our
understanding of the world.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:14.900
We need to be more curious as
business people and technologists.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.580
What would happen if we stepped out of
the view of our direct customers and

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:23.560
interviewed people who are impacted
by the second-degree effects

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.850
of emerging technology,
what might we learn?

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:32.710
There's a sort of historical
hubris here in Silicon Valley.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:37.850
There's a sense that the things that we're
building are going to change the world,

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.110
and we know exactly how and why.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.890
Whenever I hear a tech CEO or
a startup founder or

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:49.710
a venture capitalist talk about how their
company is going to change the world,

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.170
I can't help but think about my
favorite little nugget of history.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:58.690
See, the introduction of
caffeine to the European populace

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:02.140
may have helped to spur
the Industrial Revolution.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:05.842
But there's no way that
any conquistador or

00:08:05.842 --> 00:08:11.770
a merchant could have possibly known that
as they carted bushels of tea leaves and

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.240
coffee beans across oceans and deserts.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.170
The introduction of new
goods to the European market

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:22.340
could have very easily pushed
history in a different direction.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:27.150
Highly relevant to today, the bubonic
plague, which killed every third European

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:32.080
in the middle of the 14th century,
was also a side effect of open trade.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.201
Small changes can trigger
destruction just as easily as

00:08:36.201 --> 00:08:39.718
they trigger transformation and
innovation.

00:08:39.718 --> 00:08:43.012
We need to better understand our impact,
and

00:08:43.012 --> 00:08:49.390
the questions that historians asked to
study the past can help us do this.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:54.004
Isaac Asimov once said that great
science-fiction writers don't imagine

00:08:54.004 --> 00:08:56.247
the car, they imagine the traffic.

00:08:56.247 --> 00:08:59.192
I sure wish that Uber had imagined
the traffic when they built their app.

00:08:59.192 --> 00:09:03.252
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We all need to get better at imagining

00:09:03.252 --> 00:09:07.440
and fixing the traffic that
our innovations build.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.165
And the study of history can help
us ask the right questions so

00:09:11.165 --> 00:09:15.925
that we're building a future that we all
actually want to live in, thank you.

00:09:15.925 --> 00:09:17.749
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:17.749 --> 00:09:22.549
[MUSIC]

